          "question": "e-8802",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "Let $f(x)=x^4 - 6 x^3 + 12 x^2 + 6 x - 13.$ Since $x_1=-1$ and $x_2=1$ are roots of $f(x),$ then according to the factor theorem, \n\n$$\n(x+1)(x-1) = x^2-1\n$$\n\nis a factor of $f(x).$ \n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2-1$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+6x-13\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+13{x}^{2}+6x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,+6x}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,13{x}^{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,-13}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2-1)(x^2-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve $x^2-6x+13=0$ by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+{3}^{2})-{3}^{2}+13 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to our original equation are $x=\\pm 1$ and $x=3\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$",
