        {
          "question": "e-5797",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we let $f(x) = x^4 + 13 x^2 + 36.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and $x_1=2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overline{x_1}=\\overline{2\\textrm{i}}=-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$\\big(x-2\\text{i}\\big)\\big(x-(-2\\text{i})\\big)$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2\\text{i})(x-(-2\\text{i})) & =(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by $x^2+4$ using long division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}+13{x}^{2}+36\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,9{x}^{2}+36 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{9{x}^{2}+36}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x) = (x^2+4)(x^2+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve $x^2+9 = 0{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=\\pm 2\\textrm{i}, \\, \\pm 3 \\textrm{i}.$",
